<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000656> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Satyrism"@en .
<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000656> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Satyromania"@en .
<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000656> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Sexual hyperesthesia"@en .
<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000656> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Sexual hyperversion"@en .
<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000656> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Term used to describe a level or frequency of sexual desire that is perceived as excessive within a given culture. Though the medical community has not reached a consensus as to whether hypersexuality should be included as a clinical diagnosis, doctors and psychologists have historically diagnosed LGBTQ+ people with hypersexuality (or other related labels) in order to wrongly treat their identities and desires as medical disorders. Should only be used in a historical context or if an individual self-identifies with the term."@en .
<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000656> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#narrower> <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001284> .
